Phases 1 and 2 are implemented, both gates are green, and the work is committed as `43903f9c9` on `w2_stale-race`.

**What the change does:** a new package `internal/jsonlpub` owns the JSONL file and its metadata as one unit. Publishing takes a per-file lock, snapshots the dirty rows *before* building the issue set, records a pending hash *before* the rename and promotes it after, then clears only the rows that did not change while the export ran. Readers get a four-state verdict; anything other than "fresh" is re-sampled under the publish lock, so a reader straddling a publish no longer calls a healthy repo diverged. Importers hash the bytes they actually parsed. Every reader/writer (daemon export/import, CLI export/import/sync, autoflush, RPC server, staleness checks) now goes through it, and non-canonical targets no longer touch canonical metadata or dirty flags.

